we all know netflix is massive they were  the og streaming service that destroyed  blockbuster and gave cable tv providers  a run for their money but did you know  that netflix was not only a strong  performing stock but the best performing  stock in the s p 500 for an entire  decade they certainly didn't anticipate  a global international launch you know  all at once in in 2013. those are the  things that drove the stock higher if  you invested money in netflix at the  start of 2010 you would have nearly 100  extra money at the peak for perspective  with amazon you would have roughly 33  extra money and with apple you would  have roughly 25 extra money considering  this stellar performance netflix is  often grouped with the top tech  companies in the world even though it's  only a fraction of the size once the  pandemic hit netflix stock just  skyrocketed even more as people binge  shows and movies on netflix as they  didn't know what else to do  but over the past couple of months  netflix hasn't been doing so hard in  fact netflix is down over 50 within just  the last four months which correlates to  a 150 billion loss in market cap this  makes netflix the fifth worst performer  in the s p 500  so here's how netflix went from being  the number one stock in the s p 500 for  a decade to being part of the bottom one  percent all within a matter of months  one of netflix's most deep-rooted  problems is that they're dependent on  the traditional tv and movie industries  for content in other words netflix's  ability to have a large and diverse  library of content is extremely  dependent on how many licensing deals  they're able to make with production  studios now netflix has been well aware  of this since the very beginning and  that's why one of their biggest focuses  has been producing netflix originals  over the past decade they have pumped  out several phenomenal series like  bojack horseman house of cards and of  course squid game aside from these  originals they've also bought tremendous  shows like money heist and lucifer but  at the end of the day netflix's original  content and strategic purchases only  account for a fraction of their entire  library this means that if production  studios were to boycott netflix for one  reason or another netflix would lose  most of its content and probably a  significant portion of their subscribers  from the very beginning many studios  have refused to license their content in  netflix and with the time the proportion  of studios that refuse to work with  netflix has only increased due to the  rise of competitors by far the biggest  competitor against netflix is of course  disney plus disney didn't even launch  the streaming service till november of  2019 or just over two years ago but  despite the recent entrance into the  market they already have nearly 130  million subscribers  for perspective netflix currently has  221 million subscribers so netflix does  still have about 90 million more  subscribers but if disney was able to  make up half the ground within two years  imagine where they'll be within five or  ten years  it looks to me like it's just a matter  of time until disney plus surpasses  netflix in subscribers and the key  reason for this is that disney is a  full-on production company first they  have walt disney production studios with  all the classics including aladdin alice  in wonderland frozen and the moana then  they have pixar which includes seoul  coco with the incredibles cars finding  nemo inside out toy story and basically  every other animated movie that we grew  up with and then they have a marvel  which includes the entire marvel  cinematic universe from iron man to end  the game the only thing they're missing  is a few spider-man movies and then they  also have the entire star wars universe  and we can't forget about the  mandalorian and then they also have a  large library from national geographic  and then on top of all of that they have  a few oddball movies too like avatar  disney owns the full rights to almost  everything we covered with that being  said disney isn't dependent on licensing  from anyone because they already own  everything in other words disney is a  production company that launched the  streaming service while netflix is a  streaming service that does some  production and as more production  companies launch their own streaming  services like paramount plus it's just  going to become harder and harder for  netflix to retain content and  subscribers  moving on to netflix's second biggest  issue we have the pandemic now this  might be a bit confusing as the pandemic  was originally hailed as a golden ticket  for stay-at-home businesses like netflix  but long term i think the pandemic was  actually negative for netflix for two  reasons with the first being consumer's  openness to try other streaming services  here's the thing most people who are  into streaming shows in the movies  already had a netflix subscription  before the pandemic started what they  didn't have was a disney plus or a  paramount plus or a hulu subscription  and the main reason for this was that  they didn't want to pay for more than  one streaming service on a regular basis  when they were stuck at home though it  made a lot more sense to pick up a few  more subscriptions now to be honest most  of these guys have likely gotten spoiled  with having all these subscriptions and  they won't be cancelling any of them  anytime soon and this includes me as  well but for people who actually have a  social life and to do things other than  just watching youtube and movies it may  not make sense to pay for all these  streaming services and they may cut back  down to just one this brings up the  question of which subscriptions will  they cancel but really this doesn't even  matter because the net gain will always  be in favor of the competition for  example disney plus has gained 100  million subscribers since the start of  the pandemic while netflix has gained 40  million subscribers now let's say 10  million subscribers who have both  subscriptions decide to cut down to just  one even if all of them choose to stay  with netflix disney plus had a net gain  of 90 million subscribers which is over  two times netflix also realistically  this split will likely be closer to 50  50 and might even lean in disney's favor  moving on to the second reason that the  pandemic was bad for netflix in the long  run we have the seriousness of the  competition before the pandemic the  streaming business was simply in  addition to many production companies  businesses but during the pandemic  streaming became one of their top  priorities take disney for example  before the pandemic they were  consistently launching movies that  pulled in over a billion dollars at the  box office and they had theme parks all  around the world but when their revenue  from theatrical releases and theme parks  go to zero overnight where do you think  they're gonna shift their focus the  answer is obvious streaming many big box  films like solo completely skipped the  theaters and launched directly onto  streaming services and it's not just  disney either this scenario is  applicable to all production studios all  of them had much more to gain from the  pandemic and they all started taking  streaming much more seriously so the  pandemic was actually relatively harmful  for netflix  even if the pandemic didn't happen and  netflix didn't have a dependency issue  they still have a massive hurdle across  which is sustained growth here's the  thing netflix has basically put all  their eggs into the basket of video  streaming and this makes sense because  they need to focus on maintaining market  share against production studios but  let's say they do it instead of losing  to disney plus let's say disney and  netflix become a duopoly within the  streaming industry what happens next  well the answer is market saturation  sooner or later everyone who wants a  streaming subscription will already have  an epic subscription you could argue  that netflix has already reached this  point given that they miss subscriber  growth targets and are forecasting weak  growth moving forward themselves but  even if you don't think netflix has  reached market saturation quite yet how  much more could they really grow 300 400  500 million subscribers  even if they're able to grow at a half a  billion subscribers that's only a little  more than a 2x from where they are today  now this isn't a bad thing as companies  mature they obviously can't put up the  same growth numbers but with that being  said these companies should also no  longer be valued as growth companies for  much of netflix's history their pe ratio  has hovered well above 100 and even with  the recent decline they've maintained a  pe ratio over 30. so maybe the recent  decline is just the market adjusting to  the fact that netflix is no longer the  insane growth company that it once used  to be every maturing company faces this  same issue sooner or later some of them  learn to diversify and grow through  alternate streams of revenue while other  companies just stagnate for a while at  the top before they start declining as  more innovative companies take over the  market apple for example faced the same  issue in the mid 2010s iphone sales were  not only stagnating but declining at  first apple tried to combat this by  raising prices on their iphones to  increase profit margins and that's kind  of what netflix is doing right now  but the thing to note is that even apple  realized that there was a limit to how  much they could raise prices and they  would actually lower their launch price  with the iphone 11. so netflix can't  just keep raising prices to substitute  for growth for apple the answer to  slowing growth was focusing on services  like apple music apple care icloud apple  tv and so on and so forth in other words  the key was to offer more value to  existing customers as opposed to trying  to gain new customers netflix is trying  to do this as well with services like  netflix games but the problem with such  endeavors is that they're usually hit or  miss sometimes a new service is exactly  what customers were looking for other  times not so much so the market doesn't  want to give netflix too much credit  until it's proven that they have a  future in something other than just  video streaming which they've nearly  saturated  looking forward it's not like netflix is  going anywhere anytime soon we can't say  that netflix is too big to fail given  that we've seen far larger empires  getting wiped out time and time again  but given netflix's size this is  definitely not something that would  happen overnight if netflix were to fail  it would probably be similar to  something like dish network it would  start with a decade of stagnation  followed by a decade of subscriber  losses to the competition followed by  the decade in which netflix would  actually collapse so netflix has at  least two decades to figure something  out here whether that's expanding their  services into gaming and live streaming  or whether that's becoming a full-on  production studio themselves  given how much time netflix has you  would think that they're safe but then  again you could have said the same thing  about dish 10 or 15 years ago now  personally i think netflix is a much  more innovative company than dish after  all netflix is more of a tech company  than a media company so i'm sure they'll  come up with some creative solutions  especially with reed hastings at the  helm but only time will tell who do you  guys think will win the streaming wars  comment that down below  also drop a like if you're thankful that  netflix pioneered streaming and of  course consider checking out our  international channels to watch our  videos in other languages and consider  subscribing to see more questions  logically answered but until then i'm  hari 